WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.345
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.127 --> 00:00:08.710
ELISSE ROCHE: My
name is Elisse Roche,

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:11.530
and I lead our data for social
and environmental impact

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.600
initiatives here
at Google Cloud.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.970
And I'm very excited to
talk to you all today

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.430
about the circular
economy, sustainability,

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:22.120
and the opportunities to advance
sustainability with technology.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.310
And also, to kick off an
exciting panel discussion

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:25.220
as well.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.060
Now, before we actually jump
into the circular economy

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.180
more specifically
and in particular,

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.680
want to take a step back and
actually talk a little bit more

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.620
about what
sustainability really is,

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:40.230
why are we talking about
why is it important today.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.900
So kind of setting the stakes,
in 2018, the global demand

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.960
for resources was 1.7
times what the Earth

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.520
can support in one year.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.860
Between our agricultural,
energy, and consumption

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.140
practices, we are currently
consuming more resources

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.265
than the Earth can support.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.510
A circular economy aims to
redefine growth and focus

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:03.870
on positive society-wide
benefits in three key ways,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:05.760
by designing out
waste and pollution,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.190
by keeping products
and materials in use,

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.860
and even regenerating
natural systems.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.110
Now, what's really at the
core of the circular economy

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.050
is this idea of
decoupling economic growth

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.250
from resource use.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.700
And it also has the opportunity
to generate $4.5 trillion

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.510
of new economic output,
especially when considering

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.490
the advances in technology
that we have today,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:35.118
considering machine learning
and AI in particular.

00:01:35.118 --> 00:01:36.410
But what is a circular economy?

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:39.710
What are tangible examples
of a circular economy?

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.050
Well, according to the
Ellen MacArthur Foundation,

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.960
who developed the diagram
that you see pictured here,

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.460
a circular economy seeks
to rebuild capital,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.390
whether that is financial,
manufactured, social,

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:51.620
or natural.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.510
This allows, then,
for a continuous flow

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.490
of technical and
biological materials

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.955
or nutrients into
these value circles.

00:01:57.955 --> 00:02:00.230
So as you can see,
they're quite broad.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.360
There are agricultural inputs.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.590
There are electronic inputs.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.350
And as a result,
the circular economy

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.420
can take a variety
of shapes and forms.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:14.490
It can be a bio-based material
for single-use food containers.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.140
So what is pictured
here is actually

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.277
an example of a disposable cup
made from the cassava root.

00:02:19.277 --> 00:02:21.360
And so while this is more
environmentally friendly

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.300
and compostable, it actually
is additive to the environment

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.870
as well, and helps to
promote soil regeneration.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:33.700
A circular economy can also be
a rooftop hydroponic-based farm,

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.670
where 1 acre of
previously-unused roof space

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.525
can feed up to 2,000 people
per year with fresh produce.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.710
And a circular economy is
also an e-recycling model

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.450
for electric vehicles.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.160
As we know, there's
an increasing demand

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.620
for electric vehicles,
and that is also

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.960
matched by an increasing
demand for the batteries that

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.010
power them.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.600
And there's then
the question of,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.570
how can we recycle the
precious minerals, materials,

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:05.320
et cetera, that went into
making those batteries?

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.930
But what are Google
Cloud and SAP

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:09.440
doing around sustainability?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.803
How are we driving innovation?

00:03:12.803 --> 00:03:14.720
Well, looking at the
partnership more broadly,

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.925
Google Cloud and SAP announced
our partnership in 2017

00:03:18.925 --> 00:03:20.300
with the goal of
giving customers

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.060
the best of both worlds,
essentially unmatched business

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.760
expertise, industry-leading
cloud infrastructure,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.560
and machine learning solutions.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.370
So together, we provide the
flexibility and the performance

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.280
necessary in order to
drive impactful decisions

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.240
for customers around the world.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.870
We work together,
and we work for you.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.010
Now, in addition to this, we can
also help organizations scale

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.090
sustainably.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.160
From Google sustainable
data centers,

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.980
where 100% of
global energy use is

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:53.570
matched with renewable energy,
to the 91% landfill diversion

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.880
rate that we have
achieved in 2017.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.070
And looking at SAP and
their sustainable solutions,

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.570
what they're working towards.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:02.120
Making wind energy more
cost-effective, for example,

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:07.350
with customers, and also helping
to simplify risk management.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.780
Overall, together, we
believe that investing

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.640
in environmentally
responsible management

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:15.300
means investing in a
collaborative future.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.050
And that is where Circular
Economy 2030 comes in,

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.760
where Google Cloud and SAP--

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:22.460
and now you-- can come
together to create

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.990
a more sustainable world.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:28.420
Circular Economy
2030 is a contest.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.330
It is a sustainability contest
for social entrepreneurs, where

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.190
we asked applicants
around the world

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:36.130
to submit a proposal for
a revenue-generating idea

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.140
that advances a circular
economy and also

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:41.620
SDG 12, one of the United
Nations Sustainable Development

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.740
Goals that range from taking
climate action to promoting

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:45.970
gender equality.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:49.000
SDG 12 is focused on responsible
consumption and production

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.340
in particular, and
so it's very closely

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:55.630
tied to the concept of
the circular economy.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:58.420
From the applicants, the top
five eligible submissions

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.510
are to receive funding,
Google Cloud Credits, and also

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:02.995
mentorship opportunities.

00:05:02.995 --> 00:05:06.190
And there's also an additional
phase of the competition that

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:09.430
is taking place this week,
an in-person hackathon here

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.220
in San Francisco this weekend,
where the finalists will

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:13.870
be competing for the
opportunity to win

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:18.160
the grand prize of $200,000
and in-kind benefits

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.470
across funding, and
Cloud Credits in addition

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.220
to the mentorship opportunities
and the Google Cloud

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:26.260
for Startups boot
camp experience.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.720
And we're not doing this alone.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.710
It's not just Google
Cloud and SAP.

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.820
We're also working with
leading IGOs and nonprofits

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.820
in this space, including
UN Environment, the Global

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.070
Partnership for Sustainable
Development Data,

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:45.110
and the Ellen
MacArthur Foundation.

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.510
So with that, we're about
to announce the finalists.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:52.480
I'd like to welcome my colleague
Jim from SAP to the stage.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:53.480
JAMES SULLIVAN: Awesome.

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:54.580
Thank you so much, Elisse.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.960
ELISSE ROCHE: Wonderful.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.400
So the finalists.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.110
A quick note before we dive in.

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:02.410
We received over
250 applications

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.528
from over 50 countries
around the world,

00:06:04.528 --> 00:06:05.570
which is really exciting.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:10.240
So the fun fact is from every
continent except Antarctica.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:11.335
JAMES SULLIVAN: Exactly.

00:06:11.335 --> 00:06:12.250
ELISSE ROCHE: Which is great.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.720
And definitely
spent a lot of time

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.010
reviewing these applications.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:16.510
Worked really hard
on this also with

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:18.670
an interdisciplinary
and cross-industry panel

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.270
of expert judges, and are happy
to introduce them all to you

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:24.650
today.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.190
So the first finalist, the
first social entrepreneur

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.710
is Ann Runnel from
Reverse Resources

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:31.990
focused on textile waste.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.420
Reverse Resources' vision
is to help textile material

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:38.493
flows reach their highest
environmental potential value

00:06:38.493 --> 00:06:39.910
so that the need
for new materials

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:43.952
can be minimized globally.

00:06:43.952 --> 00:06:44.910
JAMES SULLIVAN: Thanks.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.650
The second finalist
is Dysmus Kisilu,

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:49.870
and his company is Solar Freeze.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:53.560
They are pioneering
portable cold storage units

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:57.580
powered by solar energy for
small-scale rural farmers

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.207
of perishable product.

00:06:59.207 --> 00:07:01.540
So it's a company that's
looking at the food waste topic

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:04.630
from a developing
country perspective,

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.990
and also converts the
waste into useful energy

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.060
and bio-alkanol, so
exciting project.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:12.400
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.267
Next is Jasmine Crow, founder
of Goodr, focused on food waste.

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:18.100
The mission of Goodr
is to reduce food waste

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.250
by connecting businesses that
have surplus food to those

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.878
who need the meals.

00:07:23.878 --> 00:07:25.810
JAMES SULLIVAN: And
fourth is Michael Groves.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:27.580
The company is
Topolytics, which has

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.790
a smart grid for the world's
waste using big data,

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.760
mapping, and analytics to
make waste management more

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.760
transparent, more efficient,
and more effective,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:40.231
both commercially and
for the environment.

00:07:40.231 --> 00:07:42.880
ELISSE ROCHE: And then there
is Patrick Peter, founder

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.243
of Circunomics,
focused on e-waste.

00:07:45.243 --> 00:07:47.410
The mission of Circunomics
is to maximize efficiency

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.660
for the circular battery
economy with the goal

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:50.890
of achieving zero waste.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.900
And with that, we'd like to
welcome the remaining panels

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:00.720
