00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:21.355
and to be partnering with
this exciting initiative.

00:12:21.355 --> 00:12:22.730
And thank you all
for being here.

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:25.600
I know it's a hard
a decision to--

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.380
with all the amazing
panels going on.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:30.910
So thank you.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:33.250
I am the community
engagement manager

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:36.610
for the Global Partnership for
Sustainable Development Data.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:39.010
And we are a global
network committed

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:42.550
to ensure that we have the
necessary data to achieve

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:44.860
and monitor the SDGs.

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:49.600
We bring together governments,
civil society, multilateral UN

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:54.580
agencies, and the private sector
together to collectively solve

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:59.390
problems and gaps around the
production and use of data.

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:03.120
We don't provide the data
ourselves, or collect data,

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.720
but rather, we provide strategic
partnerships, advocacy,

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:11.380
technical assistance,
and pilot initiatives

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:15.730
with partners from the national,
regional, and global level.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:19.810
We strive to reduce
technical barriers, but also

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:21.255
political ones.

00:13:21.255 --> 00:13:23.535
Thanks.

00:13:23.535 --> 00:13:24.410
JAMES SULLIVAN: Cool.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.150
So I have a confession to make.

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:27.280
I'm selfish.

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:29.233
We're not live
streaming this, are we?

00:13:29.233 --> 00:13:30.400
ELISSE ROCHE: It's recorded.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:31.525
JAMES SULLIVAN: Oh, we are.

00:13:31.525 --> 00:13:32.860
Good.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:34.430
So I grew up in
upstate New York.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:35.450
I love the outdoors.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:36.280
I love the winter.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:37.000
I love skiing.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:39.070
Kate and I just had a
wonderful conversation.

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:41.200
I got involved in
the climate change

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:43.000
discussions probably
20 years ago,

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.220
because I love those
outdoor spaces,

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:47.980
and I'd like them to be
available for my lifetime.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:50.330
But I've also had some of the
best times with my family,

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:53.110
with my kids in the mountains,
hiking around, skiing.

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:55.990
And I want those spaces to be
available in their lifetimes

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:57.260
as well.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:00.250
The second point, my
daughter is an athlete.

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:01.600
Has rowed crew for many years.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:05.830
She spends four or five hours a
day on the water in the rivers,

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:08.980
and comes back with a report
on what's floating down

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:11.090
the river that particular day.

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.210
So two really sobering
statistics on climate

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:16.900
change in the US
alone, $1.5 trillion

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:21.370
in damages from
natural weather events

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:25.463
and disasters related to
extreme weather around that.

00:14:25.463 --> 00:14:27.880
And then for plastics, I think
everybody's heard the stat,

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:31.970
there may be more fish than
plastic in the ocean by 2050.

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.480
So on a personal level,
I'm doing this for my kids,

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:35.860
for our planet.

00:14:35.860 --> 00:14:38.480
But it also needs to make
a lot of business sense.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:42.610
So from an SAP perspective,
for the past 47 years,

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.290
we have been managing--

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:47.920
had the privilege to manage our
customers' financial data flows

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:49.150
and material data flows.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:53.500
We run-- 77% of
financial transactions

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:56.200
float through our
systems on a daily basis,

00:14:56.200 --> 00:14:58.970
typically close to
80% of material flows,

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:01.990
whether sneakers, whether
plugging in your iPhone,

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:02.960
whether that.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.810
And if we can merge
that data along

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:07.870
with environment and social
data to allow businesses

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:11.230
throughout the world to make
better business decisions,

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:14.880
we can really make a huge
change in the world, so.

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:15.880
ELISSE ROCHE: Wonderful.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:17.650
Thank you for the
introductions and sharing

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:19.960
more about your interests
and background in this space.

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:23.020
So another question for you
about the circular economy.

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:24.790
It's a tough one to define.

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:28.720
How would you define a circular
economy in a simplified way,

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:30.670
and how can the
circular economy help

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:32.860
to navigate the often
inverse relation

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:39.173
between economic growth and
environmental degradation?

00:15:39.173 --> 00:15:40.465
Whoever wants to jump right in.

00:15:40.465 --> 00:15:41.490
JAMES SULLIVAN:
[CHUCKLES] An easy one.

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:42.290
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah.

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.460
KATE BRANDT: Well,
I have to say,

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:46.650
I like the definition
of the circular economy

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:49.080
that we've gotten from the
Ellen MacArthur Foundation

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.990
that it's about keeping
products and materials in use,

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:53.550
designing out waste
and pollution,

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:55.590
and regenerating
natural systems.

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:59.250
I think that, to me, feels
like a fairly concise way

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.690
of conceptualizing
what I know can

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:04.090
feel like a very complex topic.

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:07.590
And I really appreciate
also the element-- as Jim

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:10.200
was saying-- of the economy
in circular economy.

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:13.110
And Elisse shared some great
statistics that I often

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:16.350
cite as well, particularly the
Accenture study that showed us

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:19.500
that there's $4.5
trillion of value

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:21.520
that can be realized by 2030.

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:23.850
And what I love so much
about this competition--

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:26.220
and our fantastic winners
and all of you who applied--

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:28.650
is you're showing
this is a reality.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:32.400
You're developing new
businesses, new approaches

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:36.720
that are decoupling growth from
our use of natural resources.

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:39.660
You're showing that
we can build on all

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:42.060
of the great work,
innovation that's taken place

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:44.760
over the last 200 years, but
chart an entirely different

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:46.508
course forward that
is one of abundance

00:16:46.508 --> 00:16:48.300
and one of a totally
different relationship

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:49.570
to natural resources.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:52.092
So I really commend all the
great work you guys are doing,

00:16:52.092 --> 00:16:53.550
and I think it's
really showing how

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.740
we can put this
theory into practice

00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.315
in a really powerful way and a
way that makes business sense.

00:17:00.315 --> 00:17:01.210
DARIEN STURGES: Yeah.

00:17:01.210 --> 00:17:04.660
I would add to that by saying,
at the foundation, at least,

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:08.470
we tend to shy away from
defining specifically

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:10.329
what the circular economy is.

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:12.069
What we want to do
instead is provide

00:17:12.069 --> 00:17:15.640
more of a direction,
and especially

00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:18.252
a positive direction.

00:17:18.252 --> 00:17:19.960
Elisse mentioned the
inverse relationship

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:22.900
between growth and
resource use, and we

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:28.000
want to provide more of a
positive framework that allows

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:30.700
for resource use without--

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:34.510
or I would say, a better
use of the resources we have

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:37.790
is probably another
way of defining it.

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:41.800
And I think the other slight
differentiation I would make

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.800
is that we think
about circular economy

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:48.790
as an upstream and a
systems design issue,

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:53.570
rather than just managing the
symptoms of the problem, waste,

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:54.980
for example.

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:58.030
To us, it's the
choices you make early

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:02.060
on in the process,
the materials you use,

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:06.250
whether or not you design
for single-use servings,

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:11.020
such as the water
bottles below us.

00:18:11.020 --> 00:18:14.080
That has impact.

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:19.060
And making those choices
early on in the process

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:21.340
has a much bigger
exponential impact

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:25.120
than just trying to figure out
what to do with the plastic

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:29.140
that we pump out every year
at the end of its life.

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:31.960
The final differentiation
I would make

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:35.800
is thinking about
things like consumption

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:37.360
in a slightly different way.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.330
For instance, we
tend to even shy away

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:45.150
from saying "consumption,"
thinking that you don't really

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:48.010
consume anything
unless you eat it.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:51.570
So thinking about the resources
that businesses or individuals

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:55.650
use as materials that
are going to be used over

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:58.680
and over again, or at least
can be used over and over again

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.860
rather than simply
consumed, I think

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:05.900
is another way of at least
directionally getting

00:19:05.900 --> 00:19:08.791
at what we mean by the
term circular economy.

00:19:11.497 --> 00:19:14.200
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN: I
think what's interesting

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:17.530
about circular economy is
that it seems something

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:19.690
that's kind of revolutionary.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:22.450
And in the end, it's
because we married to idea

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.590
that the way we produce right
now is the only possible way.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:29.920
We are just so anchored
on this paradigm,

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:33.610
and I think it's very
interesting to see how many

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:38.720
like these five finalists are
attempting to change that.

00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:42.020
And I think sometimes,
it's also about--

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:48.260
we think the solutions are only
going to come from one sector.

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.410
For example, government
pushing policies

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:52.720
and pushing everyone to comply.

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:54.530
But it's not about that.

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:58.420
It's a win-win in which,
actually, businesses

00:19:58.420 --> 00:20:03.190
can benefit from
inspecting their processes

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:08.020
and making conscious decisions
about how to reduce costs,

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:13.120
their waste, their impact, and
how to think of how they insert

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:15.400
themselves in a
broader ecosystem,

00:20:15.400 --> 00:20:19.981
and how they impact themselves
and future generations.

00:20:19.981 --> 00:20:21.330
JAMES SULLIVAN: Yeah.

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:24.190
From my thought, I think it's
really a paradigm change.

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:26.790
And the reason-- so
we've been in this space

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:30.472
for years and years of ERP,
Enterprise Resource Planning.

00:20:30.472 --> 00:20:32.430
And what you're really
seeing is the definition

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:34.020
of enterprise changing.

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.540
It used to be, if you did a
good job in your own four walls,

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:38.130
check the box, you're all set.

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:41.430
And now consumers are demanding
end-to-end transparency.

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:44.850
Was the cobalt in my battery
sourced from slave labor,

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:46.147
or from child labor?

00:20:46.147 --> 00:20:47.480
What happens at the end of life?

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:48.760
It doesn't go away anymore.

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:51.930
It goes into a river,
and it goes somewhere.

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:53.430
So from that
definition, you need

00:20:53.430 --> 00:20:55.860
to not only take responsibility
for your own actions,

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.770
but your suppliers' actions,
your customers' actions,

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:00.547
and how do you pull
all that together.

00:21:00.547 --> 00:21:02.130
And the second thing
is the definition

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.930
of a resource is
really changing.

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:06.180
In the past, it was enough
to form, fit, function,

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:06.990
and we're good.

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:07.560
End of day.

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:10.080
And now we need to know, again,
the associated attributes.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:13.090
How much water was used in
the making of this resource?

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:16.740
How much carbon was used in
the making of this resource?

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:18.780
What happens to that
resource at end of life?

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:21.060
And increasingly, I
like to think of it

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:22.522
in stocks and flows.

00:21:22.522 --> 00:21:24.480
If you look at some of
the circular statistics,

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:29.370
we're really only cycling
about 9% of materials globally.

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:31.770
And some of those maybe
should be in place

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:32.940
for a long period of time.

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:36.092
But some of those, if you
open a brand new good,

00:21:36.092 --> 00:21:38.550
and you take the packaging off,
and you throw the packaging

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:40.860
away, you've engaged
with that material

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.570
for all of five
seconds and it added

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:43.990
very little value for you.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:46.120
So it's just the way
we think about that.

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:48.150
So that's a bit of
a negative view.

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:51.270
The positive view is we're in
the age of hyper transparency

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:51.970
with data.

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:54.835
And if we can allow visibility
into those material flows,

00:21:54.835 --> 00:21:56.460
that little packaging
that I threw away

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:58.560
becomes a valuable
resource for somebody else

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:00.850
in the right place with
the right technology.

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:05.090
So I'm very optimistic about our
approach here moving forward.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:06.990
ELISSE ROCHE: Wonderful.

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:11.700
So speaking to the industry
as a whole, what sustainable

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:14.730
or circular economy
principles or practices

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:18.297
stand out to you as
particularly impactful, and why?

00:22:18.297 --> 00:22:19.380
JAMES SULLIVAN: Switch up.

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:20.206
[CHUCKLING]

00:22:20.206 --> 00:22:22.020
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN: Perfect.

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:27.240
I think sustainable development
practices that really focus

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:31.200
on the issue rather
than on the solution

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:33.780
are the ones that
could have more impact,

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:37.650
and be more successful,
and gather more acceptance.

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:40.620
For example, UN Environment,
one of the partners in this

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:44.130
initiative who cannot be
here, they developed an online

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:49.450
platform in which they help
countries to find hot spots

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:53.430
of unsustainable consumption
and production practices.

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:59.430
And I think there are many
organizations advocating

00:22:59.430 --> 00:23:05.190
for several issues, and many
making the needs visible,

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:06.540
so the information is out there.

00:23:06.540 --> 00:23:10.230
I think, sometimes, the focus
isn't in the right place.

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:14.700
So making the practices
respond to solutions,

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:17.490
and thinking of the audiences,
and who they benefit,

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:18.860
I think it's the right way.

00:23:22.377 --> 00:23:23.407
[CHUCKLING]

00:23:23.407 --> 00:23:24.782
JAMES SULLIVAN:
Which way, right?

00:23:24.782 --> 00:23:27.490
DARIEN STURGES: I would say--

00:23:27.490 --> 00:23:31.630
I'm obviously excited by a lot
of the new innovations that

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:35.140
are popping up, including the
finalists of this contest.

00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:39.250
But I think what I'm probably
the most jazzed about

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:41.650
is the new collaborations
that are popping up,

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:44.410
because when we talk about
system change, or changing

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:48.130
the paradigm, it's going
to require collaboration

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:50.588
across the value chain, but
also between the private sector

00:23:50.588 --> 00:23:51.505
and the public sector.

00:23:51.505 --> 00:23:52.480
And that's happening.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:56.977
So this collaboration with
Google and SAP is one example.

00:23:56.977 --> 00:23:58.810
The foundation is
launching a new initiative

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:00.370
around the global
food system, which

00:24:00.370 --> 00:24:03.370
is helping to spearhead a
partnership between some

00:24:03.370 --> 00:24:07.250
of the leading food brands
and four global cities.

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:09.520
So it's that spirit of
collaboration that I think

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:13.367
is really going to
drive systemic change.

00:24:13.367 --> 00:24:14.450
KATE BRANDT: That's great.

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:16.490
Yeah, I was going to touch on--

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:19.410
I'll pick up on food system.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:22.880
I think this is an area where
I see so much opportunity.

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:25.782
And Elisse showed us
the butterfly diagram,

00:24:25.782 --> 00:24:27.990
as it's called, at the
beginning of the presentation.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:31.220
And I think that whole left
side of the diagram looking

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:35.450
at the biocycle presents
such tremendous opportunity.

00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:38.960
And I know one of our finalists
today is working in this space,

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:42.788
in applying technology to reduce
food waste, which is fantastic.

00:24:42.788 --> 00:24:44.330
And this is something
that I'm really

00:24:44.330 --> 00:24:45.497
personally passionate about.

00:24:45.497 --> 00:24:48.020
This is something we've been
thinking about at Google.

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:50.150
We've done a lot of work
on applying technology

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:52.340
to food waste in
our own kitchens.

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:54.800
We're now applying machine
learning and gaining

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:56.300
new insights.

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:58.288
So I see tremendous
opportunity there,

00:24:58.288 --> 00:25:00.830
and I was thrilled to see one
of our finalists really looking

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:02.020
at that topic.

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:04.803
And the other one that I
think we don't often focus on

00:25:04.803 --> 00:25:07.220
in these conversations but
that we really like to bring up

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:09.870
is the role of safe chemistry
and healthy materials.

00:25:09.870 --> 00:25:12.890
That when we are endlessly
cycling material back

00:25:12.890 --> 00:25:15.710
into the system, we also
need to be really confident

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:18.870
that that material is safe
for people and for the planet.

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:20.990
And so that's an aspect
of the circular economy

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:22.557
that I believe is
really important,

00:25:22.557 --> 00:25:24.515
and that we have done
work with the foundation.

00:25:24.515 --> 00:25:27.720
You may have seen a paper we
published on this last year.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:29.990
But I also see that as
a really important piece

00:25:29.990 --> 00:25:30.810
of the dialogue.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:32.500
And I met some folks
in the audience

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:34.250
who are developing a
materials marketplace

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:35.903
to create more transparency.

00:25:35.903 --> 00:25:37.820
We've done a lot of work
on this, particularly

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:39.300
in the building materials space.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:41.480
But that's another aspect
to the circular economy

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.577
that I think is really
important to focus on.

00:25:43.577 --> 00:25:46.160
JAMES SULLIVAN: Yeah, I'm going
to throw the 800-pound gorilla

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:49.790
out there for
business, which is,

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:52.670
if you have a supplier that's
providing you virgin materials,

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:56.930
you can ask for form, fit,
function, price, everything

00:25:56.930 --> 00:25:59.190
exactly to your specification.

00:25:59.190 --> 00:26:01.580
If you were looking at
secondary markets for materials,

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:03.890
what if the quality
isn't what you expect?

00:26:03.890 --> 00:26:05.690
What if the quantity
that you're expecting

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:06.620
isn't what you expect?

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:08.810
What if the quantity
and quality are good,

00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:11.700
but they show up in a location
that you didn't expect?

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:14.900
So one of the proofs of concepts
we've run fairly recently

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:17.780
is really, how do you do
that at a commercial scale

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:19.910
for a large company
that's gathering

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:22.040
these secondary
market materials?

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:24.800
And how do you tie it into
integrated business planning

00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:25.830
for your business?

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:28.190
What happens if the
quality isn't quite there?

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:30.150
Can I still get away
with making my products?

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:32.030
What do I need to do
to post-process it

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:33.360
in order to do that?

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:35.330
What do I need to do
with my logistics systems

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:37.520
for just in time to get the
right quality components

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:39.340
together in the right place?

00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:41.420
So for the first
time, it's really

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:43.400
tying it to the core
business process,

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:46.200
and tying it to profitability,
as you look at that at scale.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:49.130
So I think there's a lot of
great examples of circular

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:51.360
within closed loops
within companies.

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:53.120
There's becoming a
lot of great examples

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:57.140
of closed loops within
small ecosystems-- auto

00:26:57.140 --> 00:26:59.900
manufacturers, and certain
battery recyclers, and parts

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:00.440
suppliers.

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:03.230
There's not really a good
example of open ecosystem,

00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:05.100
open loops that are happening.

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:06.870
And as we get to
material marketplaces,

00:27:06.870 --> 00:27:09.410
we really need to get to that
point of increased supply

00:27:09.410 --> 00:27:13.767
and transparency to handle some
of those risks to business.

00:27:13.767 --> 00:27:14.600
ELISSE ROCHE: Great.

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:15.487
Thank you.

00:27:15.487 --> 00:27:17.570
So pivoting a little bit
to talk about technology.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:19.130
Here we are at
Google Cloud Next.

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:22.210
We've mentioned machine learning
and artificial intelligence.

00:27:22.210 --> 00:27:24.450
But what, in your
mind, in your opinion,

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:26.720
is the role of data in
sustainable development,

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:28.580
and in sustainability
more broadly?

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:30.830
That's also one to start
with Alonso, given the Global

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:32.100
Partnership team.

00:27:32.100 --> 00:27:32.530
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN: Thanks.

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:33.822
JAMES SULLIVAN: Who knows data.

00:27:33.822 --> 00:27:34.465
[CHUCKLING]

00:27:34.465 --> 00:27:35.840
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN:
And here, I'm

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:39.020
just preaching to the choir,
but data is the lifeblood

00:27:39.020 --> 00:27:40.610
of sustainable development.

00:27:40.610 --> 00:27:44.480
It's the way we understand
the problems we have at hand,

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:47.990
where they're located,
who are they affecting,

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.450
what are the right
policies to put in place,

00:27:50.450 --> 00:27:52.520
how to course-correct
these policies,

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:54.470
and ultimately,
to achieve impact.

00:27:54.470 --> 00:28:00.590
So the data value chain
crosses across policy-making.

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:04.280
And I think that for
data to truly power

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:07.190
sustainable development,
there's two conditions that

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:08.060
need to be met.

00:28:08.060 --> 00:28:11.630
And one is data has to
be about social justice.

00:28:11.630 --> 00:28:14.900
So oftentimes, the more
marginalized people

00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:17.960
in the world aren't
counted in the data.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:23.060
They are either missed
by censuses and surveys.

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:27.380
They are hidden behind
statistics and averages.

00:28:27.380 --> 00:28:31.010
And in order for data to
be truly about sustainable

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:34.820
development, it has to
be about all and for all.

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:36.380
And the second
condition, I think,

00:28:36.380 --> 00:28:39.960
it's for data to
be a public good.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:43.310
So oftentimes, data--
or lately, data

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:45.500
is referred to as the new oil.

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:51.590
I think this framing just
conceals the power of data.

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:53.390
Data is infinite.

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:57.140
Data increases
value as it's used

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:59.490
and it's collated
with other data.

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:02.900
And if the right standards,
policies, and infrastructure

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:06.570
are in place, data can
truly be a public good.

00:29:09.513 --> 00:29:10.930
JAMES SULLIVAN:
Keep mixing it up.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:11.430
I'll go.

00:29:13.272 --> 00:29:15.230
The other thing I think
is incredibly important

00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:17.450
is getting people the right
data at the right time

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:19.242
as they're making a
decision becomes vital.

00:29:19.242 --> 00:29:20.992
You could have the
best data in the world,

00:29:20.992 --> 00:29:23.000
and if it's not there
when you're acting on it,

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:24.208
it's not going to be helpful.

00:29:24.208 --> 00:29:27.680
So our team in the UK ran a
project called the Plastics

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:28.790
Challenge last year.

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:30.830
It was a guy named
Steve Jamieson, Michelle

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:33.110
[? Hickey. ?] Great, great work.

00:29:33.110 --> 00:29:35.670
They followed consumers
around-- not in a creepy way,

00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:39.650
but in a gathering data kind
of way-- for about a month

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:42.710
and gauged their every
interaction with plastic.

00:29:42.710 --> 00:29:44.600
I get up in the morning,
I eat my yogurt,

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:46.910
I throw the aluminum lid
away, I toss the cup.

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.370
Here, I grab my plastic water
bottle, I go to the gym.

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:50.840
What do I do with that all?

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:52.730
And out of that,
through partnerships,

00:29:52.730 --> 00:29:54.980
we ran a three-day
design think and came up

00:29:54.980 --> 00:29:56.960
with a lot of cool solutions.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:58.460
But if you come up
to me afterwards,

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:02.010
I've got one app that
is really interesting.

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:05.405
And what it does is it takes
the idea of the data that's

00:30:05.405 --> 00:30:07.280
collected around this,
puts it into a machine

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:08.250
learning algorithm.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:11.150
So with your phone, you can
snap a picture of a water bottle

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:12.810
and know what it's made out of.

00:30:12.810 --> 00:30:13.710
It's PET.

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:14.300
Oh.

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:16.190
And then it'll use
geolocation to know

00:30:16.190 --> 00:30:18.620
where you are and know what
the options for recycling

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:21.390
that water bottle as a consumer
might be within that region.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:23.780
So this is up and
running in the UK,

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:26.300
and it's one example of
putting data in the right hands

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:29.150
to make a decision
at the right time.

00:30:29.150 --> 00:30:32.480
We just announced
last week, the PR

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:35.840
went out with Bumble Bee tuna,
doing blockchain traceability

00:30:35.840 --> 00:30:36.530
with tuna.

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:39.500
Again, a really cool app.

00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:43.503
And it allows you to scan
a bar code on your tuna

00:30:43.503 --> 00:30:45.170
that you're buying,
and it will show you

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:47.690
exactly where that fish was
caught, what the fisherman's

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:50.750
lifestyle was like, what the
normal catch for that product

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:52.400
is, whether it's endangered.

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:55.200
And then with the company,
the fair trade implications

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:55.700
of that.

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:57.367
Like, oh, this is a
community that we're

00:30:57.367 --> 00:31:00.770
providing gloves for so they
don't hurt their hands when

00:31:00.770 --> 00:31:01.640
the lines come in.

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:04.280
All super valuable data at
the right time as you're

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:06.780
making a consumer
buying decision.

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:08.160
And then it has to be fun too.

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:09.618
So if you go to
the next page, it's

00:31:09.618 --> 00:31:12.140
got recipes for how you use
all that delicious tuna.

00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:14.420
So it's combining
the traceability

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:17.750
and the social aspects
along with consumer aspects

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:21.318
and really interesting projects.

00:31:21.318 --> 00:31:23.050
KATE BRANDT: Those
are fabulous examples.

00:31:23.050 --> 00:31:25.570
I want to hear more
about your take a photo,

00:31:25.570 --> 00:31:28.025
identify the product.

00:31:28.025 --> 00:31:29.720
But yes, I totally agree.

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:31.513
And I think just to
build on that, the way

00:31:31.513 --> 00:31:33.430
that I think about it
is, I think we're really

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:35.590
moving from the
age of information

00:31:35.590 --> 00:31:37.060
to the age of knowledge.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:40.090
We've generated so many data
sets over the last couple

00:31:40.090 --> 00:31:41.060
of decades.

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:43.450
But now, we're able to
unlock those data sets

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:45.630
to be really actionable.

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:48.310
And I think there is
tremendous opportunity

00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:50.980
to utilize data to
empower communities,

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:53.830
to empower policymakers,
to empower individuals

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:55.570
to drive sustainable change.

00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:58.480
And one of my absolute
favorite examples of this

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:02.470
is a project that we worked on
with two wonderful NGOs, Oceana

00:32:02.470 --> 00:32:05.380
and SkyTruth, which is
called Global Fishing Watch.

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:07.420
And so this is one of
these amazing examples of,

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:09.660
we're able to take
this huge global data

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:12.490
set, which is that every
ship that is on the high sea

00:32:12.490 --> 00:32:15.503
is constantly pinging out
its location every day.

00:32:15.503 --> 00:32:17.170
And so we're able to
take that data set,

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:18.940
and with a machine
learning algorithm,

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:21.520
recognize what did a
fishing boat look like as

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:23.380
compared to a cruise
ship, or a tugboat,

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:24.780
or any other kind of vessel.

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:27.280
And then what did it actually
look like when it was fishing.

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:30.520
And then we utilized our
massive cloud compute

00:32:30.520 --> 00:32:33.880
and our geomapping capabilities
to create a real-time heat

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:37.520
map of where is global
fishing activity taking place.

00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:39.670
And then communities
have been using this data

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:41.560
to create marine
protected areas.

00:32:41.560 --> 00:32:43.885
So there's actually five
new marine protected areas

00:32:43.885 --> 00:32:45.760
that have been created
just since we launched

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:47.530
this tool a few years ago.

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:49.780
And it's enabling
communities to take control

00:32:49.780 --> 00:32:52.510
of their resources, to
drive towards conservation,

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:54.640
and to partner with
the fishing communities

00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:56.410
to also enable
them, but to do it

00:32:56.410 --> 00:32:57.740
in a much more sustainable way.

00:32:57.740 --> 00:33:00.040
So one example
that I love, but I

00:33:00.040 --> 00:33:02.770
think of really putting
that data in people's hands

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:06.375
so they can drive really
positive sustainable action.

00:33:06.375 --> 00:33:07.375
ELISSE ROCHE: Wonderful.

00:33:07.375 --> 00:33:07.858
DARIEN STURGES: I
don't have much to add.

00:33:07.858 --> 00:33:09.480
ELISSE ROCHE: OK.

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:10.480
Well, there you have it.

00:33:10.480 --> 00:33:12.304
JAMES SULLIVAN: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:12.304 --> 00:33:15.178
ELISSE ROCHE: So also a
followup on technology.

00:33:15.178 --> 00:33:17.470
So what are the opportunities
to leverage-- in addition

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:18.550
to the examples
that you shared--

00:33:18.550 --> 00:33:20.592
to leverage modern
technology, especially machine

00:33:20.592 --> 00:33:23.150
learning and AI for
a sustainable future?

00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:24.490
What can we do today?

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:26.500
I think it'd be great
to hear some insights

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:30.310
from the AI and the circular
economy report that went out

00:33:30.310 --> 00:33:32.114
just a few months ago.

00:33:32.114 --> 00:33:34.400
KATE BRANDT: Yeah, absolutely.

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:36.020
So I alluded to
this a little bit,

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:39.350
but we did a report that we
published just back in January

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:42.410
with the Ellen MacArthur
Foundation, and with McKinsey,

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:44.600
of really looking at
this intersection point

00:33:44.600 --> 00:33:46.670
of circular economy and AI.

00:33:46.670 --> 00:33:51.140
And we particularly did a drill
down into the food system,

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:53.380
and into consumer hardware.

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:55.580
And I think some of
our major takeaways

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.402
were the ways in which--

00:33:57.402 --> 00:33:58.610
as we've been talking about--

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:01.530
AI can help us to
drive new insights.

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:04.820
So for example, we
looked at the opportunity

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:06.470
to design better products.

00:34:06.470 --> 00:34:08.360
And we're starting
to see examples.

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:12.020
For instance, the
European Space Agency.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:14.820
They have been using
AI to develop better,

00:34:14.820 --> 00:34:17.030
more sustainable materials.

00:34:17.030 --> 00:34:19.969
So we're seeing that as
a tremendous application.

00:34:19.969 --> 00:34:22.520
Equally, we're
starting to see AI

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:24.900
applied in really interesting
ways to business models.

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:27.650
So some of you may know
the company Stuffstr.

00:34:27.650 --> 00:34:29.600
They're a great
circular business.

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:31.880
And they utilize
machine learning

00:34:31.880 --> 00:34:34.159
to set the price
for their products,

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:36.000
for their secondary
use products.

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:39.764
So they are able to scan, what
is the price for a new product?

00:34:39.764 --> 00:34:41.389
How did they set a
fair price, but that

00:34:41.389 --> 00:34:43.070
also enables them
to make a profit

00:34:43.070 --> 00:34:45.800
and continue to
run their business?

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:48.650
So I think we're starting
to really see that AI--

00:34:48.650 --> 00:34:50.239
and particularly
machine learning--

00:34:50.239 --> 00:34:53.659
can be applied to accelerate
these new business models,

00:34:53.659 --> 00:34:56.300
and to enable new insights.

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:59.960
And in fact, many of our
applicants for this contest

00:34:59.960 --> 00:35:01.670
really showed that
in exciting ways.

00:35:01.670 --> 00:35:05.210
So I really thought those
insights were useful.

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:07.190
And equally, we've
been experimenting

00:35:07.190 --> 00:35:09.690
on this in Google, inside
of our data centers.

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:12.350
So we took a machine
learning algorithm

00:35:12.350 --> 00:35:15.710
and applied it to our data
center cooling system.

00:35:15.710 --> 00:35:18.530
And we basically created
an AI-powered efficiency

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:19.950
recommendation system.

00:35:19.950 --> 00:35:22.700
So it looked at power,
temperature, pump speeds,

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:25.640
other data sets that we
had for how the cooling

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:27.110
system was operating.

00:35:27.110 --> 00:35:30.440
And then it would
recommend, how could we

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:31.640
operate more efficiently?

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:33.110
And then we put it on autopilot.

00:35:33.110 --> 00:35:35.570
And it was just making those
recommendations on its own

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:39.530
and getting a 30% increase in
efficiency in our already very

00:35:39.530 --> 00:35:41.023
highly-optimized data centers.

00:35:41.023 --> 00:35:42.440
And I love that
example because it

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:44.660
means we can deliver cloud
even more sustainably.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:47.450
But also, it points to
the huge opportunity

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:50.970
that machine learning presents
to optimize many other systems.

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:53.927
So those are just some
of my favorite examples.

00:35:53.927 --> 00:35:55.760
JAMES SULLIVAN: Yeah,
I think Kate's grabbed

00:35:55.760 --> 00:35:58.100
all the good ones.

00:35:58.100 --> 00:36:00.070
We're doing stuff
in similar areas.

00:36:00.070 --> 00:36:02.570
The one I would add is
logistics, and the ability

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:05.600
to move things around
much more efficiently.

00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:08.090
Look at loads, look
at the way factories

00:36:08.090 --> 00:36:10.970
operate, and core
business, which I think

00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:12.760
is vitally important.

00:36:12.760 --> 00:36:15.988
Kind of a funny aside, but
from you talking about the data

00:36:15.988 --> 00:36:17.780
centers, I got back
from our Shanghai labs,

00:36:17.780 --> 00:36:20.310
and they've got this all set
up within the coffee machine.

00:36:20.310 --> 00:36:22.820
So if your milk is running
low, somebody gets a warning,

00:36:22.820 --> 00:36:25.040
and it can predict which
machines and what milk time.

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:28.970
So simple examples like that,
of real-world experience.

00:36:28.970 --> 00:36:32.090
We've done it in our Vancouver
labs around the trash bins

00:36:32.090 --> 00:36:35.660
to know when the recycle and
when the trash is getting full.

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:37.910
And learn some
real-world results

00:36:37.910 --> 00:36:40.940
from that, like, in a mobile
environment like that,

00:36:40.940 --> 00:36:42.680
the sensors might
not be as robust

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:45.560
as you would want them to be.

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:48.740
But I think the big areas
are within logistics.

00:36:48.740 --> 00:36:50.750
Certainly, it's within
dematerialization.

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:54.578
It's looking at ways of moving
contracts around rather than

00:36:54.578 --> 00:36:56.120
moving containers
around, if you have

00:36:56.120 --> 00:36:59.747
similar things with different
companies in different areas.

00:36:59.747 --> 00:37:01.580
And I think we're really
all just scratching

00:37:01.580 --> 00:37:03.540
the surface on the potential.

00:37:03.540 --> 00:37:05.930
So this contest was
a great way to work

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:07.430
with a number of
companies that have

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:08.805
some brilliant
ideas in the space

00:37:08.805 --> 00:37:11.683
and figure out ways to
make those a reality.

00:37:11.683 --> 00:37:13.600
DARIEN STURGES: And what
I would add to that--

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:15.970
taking it back to
the very high level--

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:20.440
is, when I think about the
evolution of our foundation,

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:22.660
we spent five years
basically just trying

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:25.030
to get the message out,
creating a framework,

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:29.650
mobilizing folks
around that message.

00:37:29.650 --> 00:37:34.090
We've needed to evolve
because the debate has now--

00:37:34.090 --> 00:37:36.940
or the acceptance
level of that message

00:37:36.940 --> 00:37:42.220
has led us to need to put
those principles into practice.

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:45.430
We've said things that were,
again, very high level.

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:50.290
Things like waste plus
information equals an asset.

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:53.380
Actually putting a phrase
like that into practice

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:55.720
is an extremely difficult thing.

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:58.600
And it's only through
partnerships and interest

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:00.935
from companies like
SAP and Google,

00:38:00.935 --> 00:38:03.310
and some of the advances that
have happened in computing,

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:06.520
in AI and machine learning
over the last couple of years,

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:07.900
makes that a reality.

00:38:07.900 --> 00:38:12.740
Jim mentioned the app of
taking a photo of plastic,

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:16.990
and advancements in
image recognition

00:38:16.990 --> 00:38:23.650
allows you now to basically take
a photo of waste and rethink

00:38:23.650 --> 00:38:28.960
what that means,
or it allows you

00:38:28.960 --> 00:38:31.270
to make that link between
the physical world

00:38:31.270 --> 00:38:32.770
and the digital world.

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:35.080
And that opens up just
an enormous amount

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:39.850
of possibilities to get that
waste to places, situations

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:42.910
where it's got a value,
and by definition,

00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:44.525
isn't waste anymore.

00:38:44.525 --> 00:38:45.650
ELISSE ROCHE: That's great.

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:46.610
Thank you.

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:49.460
So also a followup
question on something

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:51.980
that we touched
on briefly before

00:38:51.980 --> 00:38:55.673
that I discussed in the
presentation earlier, the SDGs.

00:38:55.673 --> 00:38:57.340
We've been talking
about sustainability.

00:38:57.340 --> 00:38:59.230
We've been talking about
the circular economy.

00:38:59.230 --> 00:39:01.397
But digging a little bit
deeper into the Sustainable

00:39:01.397 --> 00:39:02.960
Development Goals,
and especially

00:39:02.960 --> 00:39:07.310
into SDG 12, responsible
consumption and production,

00:39:07.310 --> 00:39:10.790
what would you say are
some of the challenges

00:39:10.790 --> 00:39:13.070
unique to SDG 12?

00:39:13.070 --> 00:39:15.710
What is something that needs
to be addressed today in order

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:17.053
to achieve that goal by 2030?

00:39:17.053 --> 00:39:19.095
Would love to hear your
thoughts on that, Alonso.

00:39:19.095 --> 00:39:20.220
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN: Thanks.

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.062
Of course, Elisse.

00:39:22.062 --> 00:39:26.090
So I think what's
interesting about the SDGs is

00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:28.040
that they present a--

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:30.410
while they're individual
goals, in the end,

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:32.780
it's a comprehensive
picture of all the things

00:39:32.780 --> 00:39:36.780
we need to tackle in order to
achieve long-lasting change.

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:40.590
So all these goals are
interconnected to each other.

00:39:40.590 --> 00:39:45.560
So for example, SDG 12 cannot be
achieved if we don't also work

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:49.790
on gender equality, and
protecting the environment,

00:39:49.790 --> 00:39:55.140
and resources, and, for
example, many other things.

00:39:55.140 --> 00:39:59.990
But I think the challenges--
and I talked about our work.

00:39:59.990 --> 00:40:02.160
We think about
this all the time.

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:05.660
how it's not only about the
technical barriers, but also

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:06.620
the political ones.

00:40:06.620 --> 00:40:10.100
And I think SDG 12
has the same things.

00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:14.070
It requires a more coordination
at the national level,

00:40:14.070 --> 00:40:18.960
ensuring the right approach,
scientific evidence.

00:40:18.960 --> 00:40:21.350
Obviously, the use of
data, bringing different

00:40:21.350 --> 00:40:26.120
stakeholders together to
innovate and think of new ways

00:40:26.120 --> 00:40:28.730
to improve consumption
and production.

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:35.210
So once again, in order to work
towards a circular economy,

00:40:35.210 --> 00:40:38.810
we need to think of the broader
picture, and how all of this

00:40:38.810 --> 00:40:40.244
is connected to each other.

00:40:42.812 --> 00:40:45.020
JAMES SULLIVAN: So for the
non-geeks in the audience,

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:46.580
when you put environmental
people together

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:49.010
with technology people, you
get an awful lot of acronyms.

00:40:49.010 --> 00:40:50.740
[CHUCKLING]

00:40:50.740 --> 00:40:52.940
SDG 12, Sustainable
Development Goal,

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:56.330
is about the production and
consumption, as Elisse said.

00:40:56.330 --> 00:41:00.260
And for the geeks onstage that
have dove into the details,

00:41:00.260 --> 00:41:04.220
it has some sub-targets
on halving food waste

00:41:04.220 --> 00:41:04.880
in the world.

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:07.670
So two of our winners are
looking at the food waste

00:41:07.670 --> 00:41:11.810
is a major issue, one from a
developing country perspective,

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:13.610
one from a developed
country perspective.

00:41:13.610 --> 00:41:14.540
Same issue.

00:41:14.540 --> 00:41:17.810
Two very different solutions
to how you address that.

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:20.450
It's got a goal of
reducing chemical waste.

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:23.430
It's got a goal of
reducing waste in general.

00:41:23.430 --> 00:41:25.130
To me, the one that's
most interesting

00:41:25.130 --> 00:41:28.100
is there's a goal and an
aspiration for companies

00:41:28.100 --> 00:41:31.540
to report publicly on
sustainability data.

00:41:31.540 --> 00:41:33.350
And this is where
I think you get

00:41:33.350 --> 00:41:35.420
the biggest bang for
the buck, is actually

00:41:35.420 --> 00:41:38.450
tying financial data
for corporate reporting

00:41:38.450 --> 00:41:40.520
to environment and social data.

00:41:40.520 --> 00:41:44.060
And at SAP, we're on year
eight now of our integrated

00:41:44.060 --> 00:41:46.550
report, where we're
reporting all of that,

00:41:46.550 --> 00:41:48.050
but we're looking
at the environment

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:49.910
and the social impact
on our company,

00:41:49.910 --> 00:41:52.830
and reporting that out on how
it affects our profitability.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:54.680
So I'd encourage you
to take a look at that.

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:58.080
We just put a new report
out a couple of weeks ago,

00:41:58.080 --> 00:42:00.680
and we've got a diagram
where you can transparently

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:04.250
follow the flows of
which of our KPIs

00:42:04.250 --> 00:42:06.400
as a corporation-- not
environment and social--

00:42:06.400 --> 00:42:07.940
affect each other.

00:42:07.940 --> 00:42:10.250
And some of them, like
employee retention,

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:13.940
has a fairly big effect
on profitability.

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:18.080
If we lose 1% of employees,
it's $55 to $65 million

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:21.200
in profitability hit for that.

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:23.060
So it's a really
good way of tying

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:26.210
together environment and social
along with core financial

00:42:26.210 --> 00:42:26.840
performance.

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:29.662
And it's one that I think more
and more companies need to do,

00:42:29.662 --> 00:42:31.370
because it really
helps steer the company

00:42:31.370 --> 00:42:34.370
to make the right decision
for financial reasons, which

00:42:34.370 --> 00:42:39.676
also has a pretty big
environment and social impact.

00:42:39.676 --> 00:42:41.800
KATE BRANDT: Don't have
anything else to add.

00:42:41.800 --> 00:42:42.633
ELISSE ROCHE: Great.

00:42:42.633 --> 00:42:43.580
Well, thank you.

00:42:43.580 --> 00:42:45.830
So thinking about the
finalists, they've

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:47.270
come up in the
conversation today.

00:42:47.270 --> 00:42:49.490
You mentioned food
waste in particular.

00:42:49.490 --> 00:42:52.370
Looking at their applications
and at their proposals,

00:42:52.370 --> 00:42:55.340
what from their proposals
was particularly noteworthy

00:42:55.340 --> 00:42:56.800
for you in assessing them?

00:42:56.800 --> 00:42:59.150
And in your opinion,
what was innovative?

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:01.490
What is their potential
to drive impact at scale?

00:43:04.390 --> 00:43:06.515
JAMES SULLIVAN: Turn it
over to our esteemed panel.

00:43:06.515 --> 00:43:08.000
KATE BRANDT: Oh, yeah.

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:11.960
Well, as I said, I'm thrilled
about all the finalists.

00:43:11.960 --> 00:43:14.480
And frankly, I think there
were many other really strong

00:43:14.480 --> 00:43:15.630
applications.

00:43:15.630 --> 00:43:18.943
And I think what I really
appreciated about them--

00:43:18.943 --> 00:43:19.610
as Darien said--

00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:21.950
was just the wide
variety of applications.

00:43:21.950 --> 00:43:25.350
The geographic diversity, from
looking at the food system,

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:28.070
to looking at more technical
materials like batteries.

00:43:28.070 --> 00:43:31.340
I was really heartened
by how representative

00:43:31.340 --> 00:43:34.850
all of the finalists were of
the many different opportunities

00:43:34.850 --> 00:43:37.512
and areas that we can innovate.

00:43:37.512 --> 00:43:39.220
So I was particularly
excited about that.

00:43:39.220 --> 00:43:42.290
And as I hinted that, I've
gotten really interested

00:43:42.290 --> 00:43:43.260
in the food system.

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:45.560
And I was really excited to
see that focus in the food

00:43:45.560 --> 00:43:48.140
system, because let's
face it, a third of food

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:49.160
is getting wasted.

00:43:49.160 --> 00:43:51.280
We have to figure this out.

00:43:51.280 --> 00:43:54.780
And I think technology has
such a critical role to play.

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:56.750
And also, it was
just so heartening

00:43:56.750 --> 00:43:59.960
to see how people are thinking
about using our technology--

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:02.570
and of course, SAP's technology
too-- because we ask ourselves

00:44:02.570 --> 00:44:03.890
this question all the time.

00:44:03.890 --> 00:44:05.630
How can we utilize
Google technology

00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:07.130
to enable more
sustainable outcomes?

00:44:07.130 --> 00:44:09.088
And you all thought of
things that we had never

00:44:09.088 --> 00:44:10.080
thought of before.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:14.795
So that too was, I thought,
just really exciting to see.

00:44:14.795 --> 00:44:16.170
DARIEN STURGES:
And I would say I

00:44:16.170 --> 00:44:17.530
don't have a favorite as well.

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:21.630
I think, at the foundation,
we focus on material flows.

00:44:21.630 --> 00:44:23.920
And when you look around
the world at the important--

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:26.670
or at least the
key material flows

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:31.875
that we need to
tackle as an economy,

00:44:31.875 --> 00:44:33.750
you look at textiles,
you look at food waste,

00:44:33.750 --> 00:44:37.860
as Kate mentioned,
you look at e-waste,

00:44:37.860 --> 00:44:41.340
and you ask the question,
are we using the resources

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:44.050
that we have in the most
effective way possible?

00:44:44.050 --> 00:44:46.190
And I think all of
these applicants, all

00:44:46.190 --> 00:44:50.160
of these finalists have
figured out how to do that.

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:52.230
Or at always have
a model that gets

00:44:52.230 --> 00:44:53.910
us to start attacking that.

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:57.715
So all really cool,
all great applicants.

00:44:57.715 --> 00:44:59.965
Looking forward to see over
the next six months bring.

00:44:59.965 --> 00:45:01.145
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:45:01.145 --> 00:45:03.270
So we're getting close on
time, and definitely want

00:45:03.270 --> 00:45:05.800
to allow some time
for questions.

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:09.450
But as a lightning
round, quickly, closing

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:10.890
question for the group.

00:45:10.890 --> 00:45:12.720
What is one thing
that everyone today

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:15.420
can do to be more
sustainable and to minimize

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:17.928
their footprint?

00:45:17.928 --> 00:45:19.970
DARIEN STURGES: I was
thinking about that earlier

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:22.010
when you first threw that at me.

00:45:22.010 --> 00:45:25.280
And I think whether you're
a business or an individual,

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:28.790
every decision really comes
down to two key questions

00:45:28.790 --> 00:45:32.823
that you need to ask about
a product or a thing,

00:45:32.823 --> 00:45:34.490
whether you're producing
it or using it.

00:45:34.490 --> 00:45:37.400
One is, how is that
thing going to be used?

00:45:37.400 --> 00:45:39.860
And then two, what's
going to happen

00:45:39.860 --> 00:45:42.300
once it's done being used?

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:46.250
And I think just having
that in your head

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:49.430
can really go a long
way to helping you

00:45:49.430 --> 00:45:52.940
or your company
make better choices.

00:45:52.940 --> 00:45:54.380
KATE BRANDT: That's great.

00:45:54.380 --> 00:45:57.620
And I think, of course, so
many opportunities for us

00:45:57.620 --> 00:46:00.960
to solve these challenges and
drive solutions that work.

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:03.900
But also, I think bringing this
home with us is so powerful.

00:46:03.900 --> 00:46:05.327
And I always get so excited.

00:46:05.327 --> 00:46:07.160
I feel like I've had
so many people recently

00:46:07.160 --> 00:46:10.110
tell me how much their kids
are interested in this topic.

00:46:10.110 --> 00:46:13.130
And I think this is their
future, as Jim said.

00:46:13.130 --> 00:46:16.190
So I think bringing this home,
and engaging with your family,

00:46:16.190 --> 00:46:19.790
doing composting at home,
ensuring you're recycling,

00:46:19.790 --> 00:46:21.890
looking at bringing
green cleaning products

00:46:21.890 --> 00:46:24.800
into your home, thinking
about more sustainable ways

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:27.650
of getting to work,
or getting your family

00:46:27.650 --> 00:46:29.600
to an outing on the weekend.

00:46:29.600 --> 00:46:32.210
I think these can feel like
really small decisions,

00:46:32.210 --> 00:46:34.225
but they really do
add up, and I think

00:46:34.225 --> 00:46:35.600
it's an opportunity
for all of us

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:37.580
to lead by example
and to really partner

00:46:37.580 --> 00:46:40.190
with the next generation
and seed this behavior

00:46:40.190 --> 00:46:42.390
really early, because
if we all do this stuff,

00:46:42.390 --> 00:46:43.975
the world would
look very different.

00:46:46.910 --> 00:46:48.410
ALONSO ORTIZ GALAN:
I have to agree.

00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:52.330
It's about those
consumption decisions.

00:46:52.330 --> 00:46:57.670
And whenever confronting a new
buy or purchase, just thinking

00:46:57.670 --> 00:47:01.210
of, where is it coming
from, and how does it

00:47:01.210 --> 00:47:04.720
affect someone, either
someone who's producing it.

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:09.325
And as you said, at the end of
the life cycle of that product,

00:47:09.325 --> 00:47:10.450
where's it going to end up?

00:47:10.450 --> 00:47:14.033
But also asking, do I
really need that thing

00:47:14.033 --> 00:47:14.950
that I'm going to buy?

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:16.340
I think that's really important.

00:47:16.340 --> 00:47:21.520
And while it's hard to always
having to think and rationalize

00:47:21.520 --> 00:47:24.390
every purchase that we
make, it's important.

00:47:24.390 --> 00:47:27.890
It's those small changes
that really add up, I agree.

00:47:27.890 --> 00:47:32.920
And I think the more we can
all not get into techniques

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:36.150
like Marie Kondo, or
declutter our life,

00:47:36.150 --> 00:47:38.410
or things like that,
because we're constantly

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:41.285
asking these questions,
would be great.

00:47:41.285 --> 00:47:42.160
JAMES SULLIVAN: Yeah.

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:44.710
Since it's speed round, I
have two really quick things.

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:46.348
But one is in personal life.

00:47:46.348 --> 00:47:47.890
So I have another
confession to make.

00:47:47.890 --> 00:47:50.480
I'm a musician, and I like
collecting cool instruments.

00:47:50.480 --> 00:47:53.150
And it became a problem
a few years ago.

00:47:53.150 --> 00:47:55.390
But I've put into
place for my own

00:47:55.390 --> 00:47:57.420
purchasing a one-in
one-out rule.

00:47:57.420 --> 00:47:59.500
So if I want to buy
anything, whether it's

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:03.340
a new cymbal, or a
sweater, or something,

00:48:03.340 --> 00:48:06.130
I need to decide what I'm going
to donate in order to buy that.

00:48:06.130 --> 00:48:08.863
And it really changes your
mindset in purchasing,

00:48:08.863 --> 00:48:10.780
because I might think I
really like something,

00:48:10.780 --> 00:48:12.700
but when I think of
what I have and what

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:16.300
I would get rid of in order to
acquire that, a lot of times,

00:48:16.300 --> 00:48:18.740
I'll just say, you know,
I don't really need that.

00:48:18.740 --> 00:48:21.080
So just one personal
little interesting hack.

00:48:21.080 --> 00:48:22.540
It works with food
too at the end

00:48:22.540 --> 00:48:24.400
of the week with my daughter.

00:48:24.400 --> 00:48:26.380
We do a pasta a la refrigerator.

00:48:26.380 --> 00:48:29.050
And we go through, and we have
to invent a recipe with all

00:48:29.050 --> 00:48:30.550
of the food that
we're going to have

00:48:30.550 --> 00:48:33.130
to throw out the next day
in order to make a new dish.

00:48:33.130 --> 00:48:34.900
So there's innovative
ways to do that

00:48:34.900 --> 00:48:36.820
without sacrificing,
but just making

00:48:36.820 --> 00:48:38.080
more conscious decisions.

00:48:38.080 --> 00:48:40.157
And the final thing
I would say is,

00:48:40.157 --> 00:48:41.740
when you're making
purchase decisions,

00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:43.823
when you're giving somebody
your hard-earned cash,

00:48:43.823 --> 00:48:46.570
you have the ability to ask
hard questions and make choices.

00:48:46.570 --> 00:48:50.110
And don't always make them on
the value of the transaction.

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:52.060
Make them on the
values of the company

00:48:52.060 --> 00:48:54.053
you're transacting
with for the long term.

00:48:54.053 --> 00:48:55.720
And just those two
things will, I think,

00:48:55.720 --> 00:48:58.588
really help change behaviors.

00:48:58.588 --> 00:49:00.880
DARIEN STURGES: I guess the
final thing I would say too

00:49:00.880 --> 00:49:06.640
is that the reason why we talk
so much about systems, design,

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:08.830
and changing the system
is because we don't

00:49:08.830 --> 00:49:11.980
want people to make sacrifices.

00:49:11.980 --> 00:49:15.340
When we think about SDG 12,
or responsible consumption,

00:49:15.340 --> 00:49:18.010
or however you
want to term it, we

00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:21.550
don't want Jim to buy
less cool guitars.

00:49:21.550 --> 00:49:26.035
We just want the old guitars
to go to the right place.

00:49:29.317 --> 00:49:31.150
And if we can create
those kinds of systems,

00:49:31.150 --> 00:49:34.890
then there aren't
really bad choices.

00:49:34.890 --> 00:49:37.780
But again, that's a tall
order, and that's a huge left.

00:49:37.780 --> 00:49:42.100
So we would say that
that's a long-term project,

00:49:42.100 --> 00:49:43.860
but that's the end goal.

00:49:43.860 --> 00:49:44.860
ELISSE ROCHE: Wonderful.

00:49:44.860 --> 00:49:45.640
Thank you so much.

00:49:45.640 --> 00:49:48.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

